---
import LongForm from "../components/LongForm.astro";
import { ROUTES } from "../lib/routes";
---

<LongForm
  title="About myleet"
  pageTitle="About"
  description="How myleet chooses your practice and schedules reviews. Free and open source"
  toc={[
    ["what", "What myleet is"],
    ["ladder", "The review ladder"],
    ["curriculum", "The curriculum"],
    ["companies", "Company sets"],
    ["free", "Free and open source"],
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="what">What myleet is</h2>
  <p>myleet is a practice app for software engineering internship, co-op and new-grad coding interviews. You read a short note on a pattern, solve problems in the browser against real Python tests, and the app decides what comes back and when</p>
  <p>Your daily practice comes from your history: reviews that are due, techniques you keep missing, new problems in the order the book teaches them, and the company sets you pinned. You can practice in your browser without installing anything or creating an account</p>

  <h2 id="ladder">The review ladder</h2>
  <p>Every problem you solve goes on a ladder of growing gaps: 1, 3, 7, 14 and 30 days. When it comes back, a clean solve moves it up one rung</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Move up one rung by passing the tests without hints or a look at the solution, under the time target (15, 25 or 45 minutes by difficulty)</li>
    <li>Using a hint, looking at the solution, going over time, or giving up resets the interval to 1 day. The attempt stays in your history</li>
    <li>Passing again on the same day keeps the rung. You need to solve it on a later day to move up</li>
    <li>A problem is marked mastered when you solve it from memory after 14 days without help and within the time target. Monthly reviews follow</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="curriculum">The curriculum</h2>
  <p>The eighteen modules cover interview patterns such as arrays and hashing, two pointers, sliding window, binary search, trees, graphs and dynamic programming. Read the notes in order: they explain each technique starting from a brute-force approach, then give you problems to practice. Start with the five <a href={ROUTES.learn}>start-here notes</a> on Learn</p>
  <p>Problem statements belong to their original sources. myleet links to them and shows them for practice; it doesn't claim them</p>

  <h2 id="companies">Company sets</h2>
  <p>Each company set contains practice problems for techniques common in that company's interviews, ordered most common first. These are practice recommendations, not actual or leaked interview questions</p>

  <h2 id="free">Free and open source</h2>
  <p>myleet is free, has no ads and is open source under the MIT license. The code, the curriculum and the trainer live at <a href="https://github.com/kidskoding/myleet" rel="noopener">github.com/kidskoding/myleet</a>. The same trainer runs as a command-line app and in the browser, so your history file works in both</p>
  <p>Colors are <a href="https://catppuccin.com" rel="noopener">Catppuccin Mocha</a>, MIT licensed. The notices for the open-source work myleet ships are in <a href="/licenses.txt">licenses.txt</a></p>
  <p>Read the <a href={ROUTES.privacy}>privacy page</a> to see what is stored, and the <a href={ROUTES.terms}>terms</a> for the rules of use</p>
</LongForm>
